Track 3: Fermata for an Empty Kettle
- Story & Context: When the late-August drought finally settled over Cook County, the pirate transmitter went permanently silent, prompting the neighborhood to host a final silent potluck in an abandoned Calumet River boathouse. Musicians sat around long plywood tables scraping cold tallow off tin plates, staring at an unlit kerosene stove while heat lightning flickered in the distant haze without producing a drop of water. Blind-Step Moss set down his slide and bowed the lowest gut string of the cello for seven uninterrupted minutes, leading a half-whispered, half-shouted collective dirge: "No soup left in the pot, just the sky that forgot our frequency." By dawn, the station was gone forever, but every tape deck in the district was already copying the myth.
